The additional typeclass \lean{CompleteSpace H} makes $H$ a real Hilbert space. The named hypothesis \lean{hsc} is \lean{StrongConvexOn Set.univ m f}. The gradient in the goal is the Riesz representative of $Df(x)$, so\[ Df(x)[v]=\ip{
abla f(x)}{v}. \] The goal is\[ f(x)+\ip{
abla f(x)}{y-x}+ rac m2
orm{y-x}^{2}\le f(y). \] This is stronger than the ordinary tangent-plane inequality when $m>0$. Lean has not assumed this first-order statement: it must be obtained from the given strong-convexity hypothesis.

The rewrite \lean{norm_sub_sq_real} acts on the goal and expands\[ 
orm{y-x}^{2}=
orm{y}^{2}-2\ip{y}{x}+
orm{x}^{2}. \] The modifier \lean{at hxy} followed by the goal marker applies the following simplification both to the hypothesis and to the goal. The facts \lean{inner_sub_right} and \lean{real_inner_comm} expand the remaining difference and use symmetry of the real inner product. Up to that harmless symmetry, the normalized goal is\[ egin{aligned} f(x)+\ip{
abla f(x)}{y}-\ip{
abla f(x)}{x}\ {}+ rac m2\Bigl(
orm{y}^{2}-2\ip{x}{y}+
orm{x}^{2}\Bigr)\le f(y). \end{aligned}\] To see the exact algebra, the previous hypothesis has the left side $f(x)+\ip{
abla f(x)}{y}-\ip{
abla f(x)}{x}-m\ip{x}{y}+(m/2)
orm{x}^{2}$ and right side $f(y)-(m/2)
orm{y}^{2}$. Add $(m/2)
orm{y}^{2}$ to both sides and collect terms: the displayed goal results. The tactic \lean{nlinarith} verifies these polynomial identities and the resulting inequality. It does not need a sign condition on $m$.

The facts \lean{hxy} and \lean{hyx} apply \lean{strong_first_order} to $(x,y)$ and $(y,x)$ respectively. The norm rewrite in \lean{hxy} uses $
orm{y-x}=
orm{x-y}$. The separate equality \lean{hi} reverses the direction in the linear term:\[ \ip{
abla f(x)}{y-x}=-\ip{
abla f(x)}{x-y}. \] The left-pointing rewrite using \lean{neg_sub} rewrites $y-x$ as $-(x-y)$; \lean{inner_neg_right} then pulls that minus sign out of the inner product. This equality will align both first-order inequalities with the same displacement $d=x-y$.

The rewrite \lean{rw [hi] at hxy} changes only the first inequality. The rewrite \lean{inner_sub_left} changes the goal to a difference of two inner products. With $d=x-y$, the context now contains\[ egin{aligned} f(x)-\ip{
abla f(x)}{d}+ rac m2
orm{d}^{2}&\le f(y),\ f(y)+\ip{
abla f(y)}{d}+ rac m2
orm{d}^{2}&\le f(x). \end{aligned}\] Adding them cancels $f(x)$ and $f(y)$ and yields $m
orm{d}^{2}\le\ip{
abla f(x)}{d}-\ip{
abla f(y)}{d}$. This is exactly the rewritten goal, and \lean{linarith} verifies that linear addition and rearrangement.

The fact \lean{hfirst} is the strong first-order inequality with base $x$ and target $z$. Let $I=\ip{
abla f(x)}{z-x}$, $a=
orm{
abla f(x)}$, and $s=
orm{z-x}$. Then\[ \lean{hfirst}:\quad f(x)+I+ rac m2s^{2}\le f(z). \] The fact \lean{abs_real_inner_le_norm} gives $|I|\le as$. Its use through \lean{neg_le_of_abs_le} produces the lower bound $-as\le I$, not an upper bound on $I$. Thus $-I\le as$. Combining these two inequalities gives\[ f(x)-f(z)\le -I- rac m2s^{2}\le as- rac m2s^{2}. \] This is the declared \lean{hgap}. The nested \lean{linarith} verifies that the signs and subtraction of the quadratic term have exactly this direction.
